  "note": "Run on 2026-10-19 on the live AwwO, in the operator’s own account and workspace, on Claude Opus 4.8, every agent working in its own sandbox computer. The first run finished 17 of 19 nodes: the tester stopped on a model provider error. Its team session is the one the page replays: four turns by three bots, each turn in its own sandbox computer, every command, file, web request and screenshot through the recorder (rec.py, which the Turn recorder node writes from its template with no model), and each turn handing the project to the next as a file. In Chrome that run’s page did not start its replay, its self-test threw, and the chat pushed the computer off the screen. The faults were in five modules: the kernel’s current() returned turns where the interface and the replay read a team session’s entry; the replay kept an object as turns, named bots by the turns’ spelling rather than by id, and dropped live events that had no time; the shared computer wrote nothing into a folder that did not exist yet; and the chat replaced its region’s class. So the second run ran the Kernel, Interface, Shared computer, Chat and Replay builders again with that in their tasks. Its Chat stopped on a model provider error, and the third run ran it again, with the assembly, Test and fix and Review: all 64 self-tests passed. The fourth and fifth runs ran the Interface builder again for what Chrome showed (the terminal in a serif face, a folded sidebar that clipped the roster, the status bar on two lines, a warning sign on three tabs), and the sixth ran the Review again so that it delivers its review as a file. The tester made no fix in any run. AwwO’s own log of the four turn agents’ tool calls shows shell commands (each through the recorder), file reads and listings and two publishes each, and one direct file write in Ivy’s turn (her 38th step of 45); every file in the team session is one the recorder found in its workspace after one of its commands. Each node shows its last run, and every node’s text is the canvas a member copies today. The delivered page is the tester’s, exactly as delivered, with no manual edits.",
  "verification": "Before publishing, the delivered page was opened in Chrome (1440×900, 1280×800 and 1024×768) with every network request blocked. It opens replaying the team session at twice its speed: the roster of Wren, Bram and Ivy with each one’s status, the group chat with each message and a chip for each command, file, web request and screenshot, and the shared computer following each event (the terminal, the editor with the file being written, the browser with the bulletin). The team session plays to its end: Wren fetches the National Weather Service forecast for Portland and writes notes.md; Bram builds bulletin.html and bulletin.css and looks at them at 1280×800 and 390×844; Ivy fetches the forecast again, checks the page and writes review.md asking for three label colours with more contrast; Bram changes them and writes CHANGES.md. A click on a chip seeks to that event and opens its file. Take over puts the team’s files in the shared computer (ls and echo into a file ran in its terminal), and Hand back returns to the replay. A message to @Bram after Connect says in this preview that the page cannot reach the network and works when opened on its own. Its self-test passes all 64 tests and rejects all 20 planted failures, as the tester reported, and leaves the page as it was. At a phone’s 390 px width the columns stack and the top bar shows only its icons; the computer is below the chat."
